meta-yocto-containers-demo
==========================

Demonstrations of building, bundling and securing OCI containers with the
Yocto Project and meta-virtualization:

  - iot-gateway-image: an IoT edge gateway that runs three Yocto Project
    Reference Containers (mosquitto, nginx, python) under Podman
  - SLSA provenance: the slsa-provenance and slsa-provenance-image classes
    generate in-toto/SLSA provenance attestations for images
  - grype and trivy: vulnerability scanners for container images

The demos are not hardened for production use. For example, the gateway's
MQTT broker accepts anonymous connections and, by default, the image allows
an empty root password (see "Login" below).


Dependencies
============

  URI: https://git.openembedded.org/openembedded-core
  layers: meta
  branch: master

  URI: https://git.openembedded.org/bitbake
  branch: master

  URI: https://git.openembedded.org/meta-openembedded
  layers: meta-oe, meta-filesystems, meta-networking, meta-python,
          meta-webserver
  branch: master

  URI: https://git.yoctoproject.org/meta-virtualization
  branch: master


IoT gateway demo
================

The gateway runs three unmodified containers from registry.yocto.io/library
as Podman Quadlet services:

  - mosquitto: MQTT broker on port 1883, MQTT over WebSocket on port 9001
  - dataproc (python image): publishes mock sensor readings to
    sensors/raw/<id>, sensors/processed/<id> and sensors/status
  - nginx-dashboard (nginx image): dashboard page on port 8080

No container image is rebuilt. The configuration files and the Python
application are installed into /usr/share/iot-gateway and bind-mounted
read-only into the stock containers.

Recipes:

  - recipes-demo/iot-gateway/iot-gateway-bundle_1.0.0.bb: bundles the three
    images, pinned by digest, with the container-bundle class
  - recipes-demo/iot-gateway/iot-gateway-assets_1.0.0.bb: configuration,
    application and Quadlet files
  - recipes-demo/images/iot-gateway-image.bb: the gateway image. It inherits
    container-cross-install, which imports the bundled images into Podman
    storage at build time
  - conf/distro/include/iot-gateway.inc: Podman, crun and systemd settings

Building and running
--------------------

1. Add this layer and its dependencies to your build:

     bitbake-layers add-layer meta-yocto-containers-demo

2. Add to conf/local.conf:

     require conf/distro/include/iot-gateway.inc

3. Build the image:

     MACHINE=qemuarm64 bitbake iot-gateway-image

   The images are multi-arch, so MACHINE=qemux86-64 also works.

4. Boot it:

     runqemu nographic iot-gateway-image

5. From the build host, watch the sensor data:

     mosquitto_sub -h 192.168.7.2 -t 'sensors/#' -v

   and open http://192.168.7.2:8080 in a web browser. The dashboard page
   loads mqtt.js from unpkg.com, so the browser needs Internet access.

Without TAP networking, forward the ports through user mode networking by
adding to conf/local.conf:

     QB_SLIRP_OPT = "-netdev user,id=net0,hostfwd=tcp::1883-:1883,hostfwd=tcp::9001-:9001,hostfwd=tcp::8080-:8080"

then run "runqemu slirp nographic iot-gateway-image" and use localhost
instead of 192.168.7.2.

On the gateway, check the containers with:

     podman ps
     systemctl status iot-mosquitto iot-nginx iot-dataproc

Login
-----

By default, root logs in on the console with no password. For a gateway that
is more than a demo, build with PACKAGECONFIG "nonroot":

  - root is locked and no account has a password, so nobody can log in on
    the console;
  - the image runs an OpenSSH server, and the user "admin" logs in with an
    SSH key;
  - admin runs commands as root with sudo, without a password, so the key
    gives root access. Keep its private key safe.

Make a key on the machine that will connect to the gateway:

     ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f ~/.ssh/iot-gateway_ed25519

and add its public key to conf/local.conf:

     PACKAGECONFIG:pn-iot-gateway-image = "nonroot"
     IOT_GATEWAY_ADMIN_SSH_KEY = "ssh-ed25519 AAAA... admin@host"

The build stops with an error if the key is missing. Then log in with:

     ssh -i ~/.ssh/iot-gateway_ed25519 admin@<gateway address>


Network services (mqtt-auth)
----------------------------

By default the dashboard is plain HTTP on port 8080, and the MQTT broker
accepts anonymous clients on ports 1883 and 9001 from any host. Build with
PACKAGECONFIG "mqtt-auth" of iot-gateway-assets to harden them:

  - the dashboard is HTTPS only, on port 8443, and asks for a login;
  - the dashboard's MQTT over WebSocket goes through nginx to the broker,
    whose plain MQTT and WebSocket listeners accept only this host;
  - other hosts use MQTT over TLS on port 8883 with a password;
  - each MQTT account may only use its topics: "dataproc" publishes,
    "viewer" and the dashboard only read.

On its first boot the gateway makes a self-signed certificate and random
passwords, so no secret is in the image. They are in /etc/iot-gateway, which
only root can read; the logins for people are in /etc/iot-gateway/credentials.
Add to conf/local.conf:

     PACKAGECONFIG:pn-iot-gateway-assets = "mqtt-auth"

Together with PACKAGECONFIG "nonroot" of the image, this is the hardened
gateway. On the gateway, as admin:

     sudo cat /etc/iot-gateway/credentials
     sudo mosquitto_sub -h localhost -p 8883 \
         --cafile /etc/iot-gateway/tls/cert.pem -u viewer -P <password> -t 'sensors/#'


